WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.545
Performance Review.

2
00:00:01.825 --> 00:00:05.729
Day 86 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Career phase.

3
00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:08.503
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.783 --> 00:00:11.663
Your rating is mostly decided before the meeting.

5
00:00:11.943 --> 00:00:16.039
It depends on what you did all year and how well your manager knows about it.

6
00:00:16.319 --> 00:00:22.655
Track your wins, write a strong self-review, and treat the conversation as planning for next year, not a verdict.

7
00:00:24.005 --> 00:00:24.858
Part 1.

8
00:00:25.138 --> 00:00:26.226
The review cycle.

9
00:00:27.476 --> 00:00:28.393
Step 1:

10
00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:33.260
All year: keep a wins file; align on goals; ask for feedback every quarter.

11
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.457
Step 2:

12
00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:37.767
Self-assessment: write it with impact and numbers.

13
00:00:38.047 --> 00:00:38.964
Step 3:

14
00:00:39.244 --> 00:00:43.297
Peer feedback: choose reviewers wisely; write good reviews for others.

15
00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:44.537
Step 4:

16
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:48.572
Review meeting: listen, clarify, discuss, agree on goals.

17
00:00:48.852 --> 00:00:49.812
Step 5:

18
00:00:50.092 --> 00:00:54.316
After: follow-up in writing; act on feedback; check in at 3 months.

19
00:00:55.666 --> 00:00:56.541
Part 2.

20
00:00:56.821 --> 00:00:58.421
All year: the wins file.

21
00:00:59.671 --> 00:01:02.700
A simple note you update every Friday (5 minutes):

22
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.927
Here is a template you can copy.

23
00:01:06.207 --> 00:01:25.151
Nov 2026. - Shipped coupon validation, then stopped ~40 thousand rupees or week leakage (PR 212). - Led incident response for checkout outage; wrote post-mortem; 2 fixes shipped. - Mentored Kavya on Kafka; she now owns the consumer service. - Praise:

24
00:01:25.431 --> 00:01:29.655
Clearest incident updates I've seen, VP Eng, Slack, 12 Nov.

25
00:01:32.505 --> 00:01:33.145
Tip:

26
00:01:33.425 --> 00:01:35.559
No surprises, in either direction.

27
00:01:35.839 --> 00:01:37.567
Ask your manager every quarter:

28
00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:41.217
If reviews were today, where would I land, and what would move me up?

29
00:01:41.497 --> 00:01:43.865
That way, the review meeting doesn't surprise you.

30
00:01:45.215 --> 00:01:46.111
Part 3.

31
00:01:46.391 --> 00:01:48.055
Writing your self-assessment.

32
00:01:49.305 --> 00:01:52.761
Use Action, then Impact, then Evidence for every point:

33
00:01:54.111 --> 00:01:55.455
Here is a quick table:

34
00:01:55.735 --> 00:01:56.866
Weak, and Strong.

35
00:01:57.146 --> 00:01:58.789
Worked on the checkout service.:

36
00:01:59.069 --> 00:02:05.533
Re-architected checkout to use the outbox pattern, cutting failed orders by 60% (dashboard link).

37
00:02:05.813 --> 00:02:06.943
Helped juniors.:

38
00:02:07.223 --> 00:02:12.343
Mentored 3 engineers with weekly sessions; 2 now independently own services.

39
00:02:12.623 --> 00:02:13.818
Fixed many bugs.:

40
00:02:14.098 --> 00:02:19.687
Closed 48 bugs, including 5 P1s; reduced support tickets by 30%.

41
00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:21.098
Good team player.:

42
00:02:21.378 --> 00:02:26.690
Unblocked the payments team twice by building a shared test harness, saving ~2 weeks.

43
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.533
Self-assessment template.

44
00:02:29.813 --> 00:02:31.392
Here is the MARKDOWN code.

45
00:02:31.672 --> 00:02:35.299
It runs to 21 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

46
00:02:35.579 --> 00:02:37.371
Key achievements (3 to 5).

47
00:02:37.651 --> 00:02:39.059
Goals from last cycle.

48
00:02:39.339 --> 00:02:40.469
Growth and learning.

49
00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:44.066
Warning:

50
00:02:44.346 --> 00:02:45.967
Common self-review mistakes.

51
00:02:46.247 --> 00:02:57.767
Listing tasks instead of impact, being too modest (I just helped a bit), being vague about misses, forgetting work from early in the year (that's what the wins file is for), writing 10 pages.

52
00:02:58.047 --> 00:02:59.370
Keep it to 1 to 2.

53
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.637
Part 4.

54
00:03:01.917 --> 00:03:02.963
Peer reviews.

55
00:03:04.213 --> 00:03:05.471
Giving peer feedback.

56
00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:07.799
Be specific, balanced and useful:

57
00:03:09.149 --> 00:03:11.027
Here is a template you can copy.

58
00:03:11.307 --> 00:03:12.160
Strengths:

59
00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.579
Meena's incident leadership during the October outage was outstanding.

60
00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:22.917
Clear updates every 20 minutes kept leadership calm, and the post-mortem led to. real fixes.

61
00:03:23.197 --> 00:03:24.029
To grow:

62
00:03:24.309 --> 00:03:29.771
In design reviews she sometimes moves to a solution before the. problem is fully discussed.

63
00:03:30.051 --> 00:03:34.467
Pausing to ask what else could we do? would bring. in more ideas from the team.

64
00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:38.618
Choosing your reviewers.

65
00:03:38.898 --> 00:03:40.882
People who saw your best work closely.

66
00:03:41.162 --> 00:03:44.853
At least one person from another team (shows cross-team impact).

67
00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:47.074
Ask them nicely, with context:

68
00:03:47.354 --> 00:03:49.082
Would you be open to reviewing me?

69
00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:52.946
It'd help if you could mention the search migration we did together.

70
00:03:54.296 --> 00:03:55.256
Part 5.

71
00:03:55.536 --> 00:03:56.581
The review meeting.

72
00:03:57.831 --> 00:03:58.706
Mindset.

73
00:03:58.986 --> 00:04:00.458
Go in calm and curious.

74
00:04:00.738 --> 00:04:05.602
Your goal is to understand the feedback and agree on a plan, not to win an argument.

75
00:04:06.952 --> 00:04:07.741
Scripts.

76
00:04:08.021 --> 00:04:08.789
Opening.

77
00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:10.008
You could say:

78
00:04:10.288 --> 00:04:11.760
Thanks for taking the time.

79
00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:17.992
I'd like to hear your feedback first, and then I'd love to talk about goals and what the next level looks like for me.

80
00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:20.259
Good review.

81
00:04:20.539 --> 00:04:21.478
You could say:

82
00:04:21.758 --> 00:04:23.379
Thank you, that means a lot.

83
00:04:23.659 --> 00:04:26.497
What would it take to get to the next level over the next cycle?

84
00:04:26.777 --> 00:04:29.209
I'd like to make sure I'm working on the right things.

85
00:04:30.559 --> 00:04:33.737
Even with a great rating, ask for one thing to improve.

86
00:04:35.087 --> 00:04:36.410
Asking for clarity.

87
00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:37.629
You could say:

88
00:04:37.909 --> 00:04:43.711
You mentioned I need to 'show more ownership.' Could you give me an example where I could have done that differently?

89
00:04:43.991 --> 00:04:46.573
I want to make sure I understand what it looks like.

90
00:04:47.923 --> 00:04:49.395
Disagreeing with the rating.

91
00:04:49.675 --> 00:04:50.613
You could say:

92
00:04:50.893 --> 00:04:52.472
I appreciate the feedback.

93
00:04:52.752 --> 00:04:55.589
I see some of it differently, and I'd like to share why.

94
00:04:55.869 --> 00:05:03.037
I led the checkout migration end to end, which was one of the team's top goals, and it reduced failures by 60%.

95
00:05:03.317 --> 00:05:05.685
Could you help me understand how that was weighed?

96
00:05:07.035 --> 00:05:08.785
Then, if it still feels unfair:

97
00:05:10.135 --> 00:05:11.073
You could say:

98
00:05:11.353 --> 00:05:13.337
I'd like some time to reflect on this.

99
00:05:13.617 --> 00:05:15.303
Could we follow up later this week?

100
00:05:15.583 --> 00:05:18.633
And what's the process if I'd like the rating reconsidered?

101
00:05:19.983 --> 00:05:20.901
Watch out:

102
00:05:21.181 --> 00:05:21.842
Don't.

103
00:05:22.122 --> 00:05:29.162
Raise your voice, compare yourself to named colleagues (But Rahul got...), threaten to quit, cry foul in team channels.

104
00:05:29.442 --> 00:05:32.301
Stay factual and calm, and put things in writing.

105
00:05:33.651 --> 00:05:35.507
Unexpected negative feedback.

106
00:05:35.787 --> 00:05:36.939
Pause and breathe.

107
00:05:37.219 --> 00:05:39.096
You don't need to respond instantly.

108
00:05:39.376 --> 00:05:40.869
Thank you for being direct.

109
00:05:41.149 --> 00:05:43.645
This is hard to hear, but I want to understand.

110
00:05:43.925 --> 00:05:45.717
Can you share specific examples?

111
00:05:45.997 --> 00:05:48.131
What would 'good' look like in the next 3 months?

112
00:05:48.411 --> 00:05:50.117
Can we check in monthly on this?

113
00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:52.897
Asking about promotion.

114
00:05:53.177 --> 00:05:54.115
You could say:

115
00:05:54.395 --> 00:05:57.382
I'd like to be considered for promotion to Staff Engineer.

116
00:05:57.662 --> 00:06:00.265
What gaps do you see between my work and that level?

117
00:06:00.545 --> 00:06:05.643
Could we write down 2 to 3 concrete goals and a timeline, and check progress quarterly?

118
00:06:06.993 --> 00:06:08.572
Asking about compensation.

119
00:06:08.852 --> 00:06:09.791
You could say:

120
00:06:10.071 --> 00:06:12.972
How does this rating translate into my compensation?

121
00:06:13.252 --> 00:06:19.311
Given my impact this year and the market for my role, I'd like to discuss whether there's room for an adjustment.

122
00:06:20.661 --> 00:06:23.157
See Salary Negotiation for full scripts.

123
00:06:24.507 --> 00:06:26.533
Questions to ask in every review.

124
00:06:26.813 --> 00:06:30.269
What's the one thing that would most increase my impact next year?

125
00:06:30.549 --> 00:06:33.899
What do you see as my biggest strength, and am I using it enough?

126
00:06:34.179 --> 00:06:36.909
How do others (peers, stakeholders) see my work?

127
00:06:37.189 --> 00:06:39.387
What does the next level look like in practice?

128
00:06:39.667 --> 00:06:41.437
How can you support me in getting there?

129
00:06:42.787 --> 00:06:43.726
Part 6.

130
00:06:44.006 --> 00:06:45.115
After the review.

131
00:06:46.365 --> 00:06:51.613
Send a short summary email within 24 hours (feedback, goals, agreed support).

132
00:06:51.893 --> 00:06:55.541
Turn feedback into 2 to 3 goals with measurable outcomes.

133
00:06:55.821 --> 00:06:57.805
Put a 3-month check-in in the calendar.

134
00:06:58.085 --> 00:07:00.027
Start next year's wins file today.

135
00:07:01.377 --> 00:07:03.254
Here is a template you can copy.

136
00:07:03.534 --> 00:07:04.409
Subject:

137
00:07:04.689 --> 00:07:06.673
Summary of our performance review.

138
00:07:06.953 --> 00:07:09.150
Hi Priya, thanks for the review today.

139
00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:10.475
To summarise:

140
00:07:10.755 --> 00:07:13.081
Strengths: incident leadership, mentoring.

141
00:07:13.361 --> 00:07:17.819
Focus areas: earlier risk communication, more design-doc writing.

142
00:07:18.099 --> 00:07:22.345
Goals: lead 2 design docs; flag schedule risks within 2 days.

143
00:07:22.625 --> 00:07:27.211
Next step: check-in on 15 Feb; promotion discussion in the next cycle.

144
00:07:27.491 --> 00:07:29.155
Let me know if I've missed anything.

145
00:07:32.005 --> 00:07:32.944
Part 7.

146
00:07:33.224 --> 00:07:35.891
When you're the reviewer (team leads and managers).

147
00:07:37.141 --> 00:07:37.951
Prepare:

148
00:07:38.231 --> 00:07:41.815
Gather evidence across the whole year, not just the last month.

149
00:07:42.095 --> 00:07:43.781
Read their self-review first.

150
00:07:44.061 --> 00:07:45.170
No surprises:

151
00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.221
Nothing in the review should be news.

152
00:07:47.501 --> 00:07:50.189
Hard feedback belongs in regular one-on-ones.

153
00:07:50.469 --> 00:07:51.685
Specific and fair:

154
00:07:51.965 --> 00:07:54.610
Use SBI (Situation-Behaviour-Impact).

155
00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:57.791
Judge outcomes and behaviour, not personality.

156
00:07:58.071 --> 00:07:59.095
Forward-looking:

157
00:07:59.375 --> 00:08:02.213
Spend half the time on growth and next-cycle goals.

158
00:08:03.563 --> 00:08:04.501
You could say:

159
00:08:04.781 --> 00:08:07.576
Your rating this cycle is Meets Expectations.

160
00:08:07.856 --> 00:08:12.677
Strength: your code quality and reliability work, especially the retry framework.

161
00:08:12.957 --> 00:08:14.408
To grow to the next level:

162
00:08:14.688 --> 00:08:18.891
I'd like to see you drive decisions in design reviews, not just contribute.

163
00:08:19.171 --> 00:08:22.029
Let's set one goal around that and check in every month.

164
00:08:22.309 --> 00:08:23.568
What support would help?

165
00:08:24.918 --> 00:08:25.793
Part 8.

166
00:08:26.073 --> 00:08:27.459
Practice conversation:

167
00:08:27.739 --> 00:08:30.022
Asking about a rating you disagree with.

168
00:08:31.272 --> 00:08:33.299
Here is how the conversation goes.

169
00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:36.096
Your rating this year is Meets Expectations.

170
00:08:36.376 --> 00:08:37.315
Thank you.

171
00:08:37.595 --> 00:08:40.432
I'll be honest, I expected a higher rating.

172
00:08:40.712 --> 00:08:43.421
Could you help me understand how it was decided?

173
00:08:43.701 --> 00:08:47.840
Your delivery was strong, but we expected more leadership in design reviews.

174
00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.059
I see.

175
00:08:49.339 --> 00:08:54.075
I did lead the checkout migration, which was one of the team's top goals.

176
00:08:54.355 --> 00:08:55.741
How was that weighed?

177
00:08:57.091 --> 00:08:58.691
The conversation continues.

178
00:08:58.971 --> 00:09:00.379
It was a big factor.

179
00:09:00.659 --> 00:09:02.558
That's why the rating isn't lower.

180
00:09:02.838 --> 00:09:03.777
Okay.

181
00:09:04.057 --> 00:09:06.745
What would Exceeds look like next year, specifically?

182
00:09:07.025 --> 00:09:10.267
Leading two major designs and mentoring one engineer.

183
00:09:10.547 --> 00:09:11.571
That's clear.

184
00:09:11.851 --> 00:09:15.457
Could we write those goals down and review them every quarter?

185
00:09:15.737 --> 00:09:16.867
Absolutely.

186
00:09:18.217 --> 00:09:19.753
That's the end of this lesson.

187
00:09:20.033 --> 00:09:24.684
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

188
00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:31.087
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

